To: <LithosAI support address>
Subject: API v1 — media input is undocumented, per-model and silently dropped; no file upload API

Hello,

I use api.lithosai.cloud/v1 and spent a day probing how it carries media input,
against deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and moonshotai/Kimi-K3 on 2026-10-05.
Image input works; audio and video do not; and the failures are of the three
kinds that cost a caller the most time: undocumented, inconsistent per model,
and silent. Four bugs, then three things I would like to see added.

Bugs

1. Image input works, and nothing documents it.

An image_url content part with an https URL, or with a
data:image/...;base64,... URL, returns 200 and the model reads the picture —
prompt_tokens 209 with the image against 14 for the same question alone. A
detail field is accepted. This is good behaviour that no one can discover: your
reference (docs.lithosai.com/openapi.yaml, info.version 2026-09-17) types a
message's content as string|array|null with no part schema. Please document the
content-part shape the endpoint already accepts.

2. Audio and video parts are accepted and then dropped — or rejected,
   depending on the model.

- moonshotai/Kimi-K3 returns 400 "Kimi-K3 supports image input only" for
  video_url, input_audio and audio_url. That is the right answer.
- deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash accepts all three with HTTP 200 and delivers
  nothing. The model is told the part is unsupported: for a video_url part it
  answers that the video "isn't accessible", for input_audio and audio_url
  that the audio "didn't come through" or is marked unsupported. An
  unreachable URL in either part also returns 200 with no load error — the same
  prompt_tokens as a reachable one (20 with video_url, 19 with audio_url,
  against 14 for the same text alone, where an image part adds about 195). So
  no media is tokenised and no URL is even fetched.

A minimal repro is one POST /v1/chat/completions whose user content is a
video_url, input_audio or audio_url part plus a text part. It returns 200 on
DeepSeek and the model answers that it cannot see or hear it.

If DeepSeek on your engine cannot take these inputs, reject them the way
Kimi-K3 does instead of accepting and discarding them. An input that is
accepted and ignored is worse than one that is refused: the model is told the
part is "unsupported", leaking a serving detail into its context, and the
caller has no error to act on.

3. Error bodies leak your server internals and misdirect the caller.

Three examples from the same session:

- Undecodable image URL, DeepSeek: 400 "An exception occurred while loading
  IMAGE data at index 0: Error while loading data ImageData(url='https://...'):
  Could not d..."
- Same, Kimi-K3: 400 "Could not decode image: cannot identify image file
  <_io.BytesIO object at 0x77f7cd34b470>"
- Unknown part type: 400 "messages.0.function-after[validate_thinking_parts_role(),
  ChatCompletionMessageGenericParam].role: Value error, 'role' must be one of
  'system', 'assistant', 'tool', 'function', 'developer', 'latest_reminder'..."

These expose a Python object repr, a heap address, and Pydantic validator and
class names, and the third points the caller at role when the real problem is
the part type (a file_id part is rejected this way too). Please return a plain
error that names the offending field and part type.

4. There is no way to know which model supports which media.

GET /v1/models and GET /v1/models/{author}/{slug} return only id, object,
created and owned_by. Kimi-K3's media policy is discoverable only by getting a
400; DeepSeek's is not discoverable at all, because it answers 200. Add per
model capability fields (for example input: text and/or image and/or audio
and/or video) to the model objects, or a capabilities endpoint.

Requests

5. Audio input. Support it, at least for models that can take it. OpenAI's
   input_audio shape — a base64 payload with a format — or an audio_url
   reference would both be fine; what matters is that a supported model
   receives the audio and that an unsupported one is refused clearly. Today
   neither happens: the part is accepted and marked unsupported on DeepSeek,
   and rejected on Kimi-K3.

6. Video input, actually served. If video is on the roadmap, say which models
   will support it and in what form (a video_url part, or frames as images);
   if it is not, remove video_url from the accepted part set so it stops
   answering 200 with nothing behind it.

7. A file upload API. There is none today: GET and multipart POST /v1/files
   both 404, and a file_id content part is rejected. The two things missing are
   an upload endpoint that returns an id (multipart POST /v1/files →
   {id, object, bytes, purpose}), with GET and DELETE alongside it, and content
   parts that reference a stored file by id, so a large image, audio clip or
   video does not have to be inlined as base64 in every request or re-fetched
   from a third-party URL. Keeping the URL reference as well is useful; the
   upload path is what makes audio and video practical.

Priority, if it helps:

- Make unsupported audio and video fail with 400 instead of 200-and-drop.
- Add the file upload API, then audio and video input.
- Document the content-part schema, including image input.
- Publish per-model media capabilities.
- Stop returning Python reprs, addresses and validator class names in 4xx
  bodies.

Reproductions are raw POST /v1/chat/completions requests with the content parts
above; I can send the full request/response pairs, including headers, on
request.

Thanks,
